          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Indian mathematicians working in the Middle Ages often composed their problems in lyrical passages. Solve the following problem, which appears in the 12th century text ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Lilavati",
              "marks": [
                "italic"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p14-q20-lyrical-text",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "O deer eyed one! Tell me, if one sixth of the number of bees in a colony entered a jasmine flower tree, one-third went to kadamba tree, one fourth flew to a mango tree, one-fifth went to a tree blooming with sampaka flowers, one-thirtieth went to a beautiful bed of lotuses bloomed by the sun’s rays and if the remaining one bee was roving about, how many total bees were there in the colony?",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "60 bees.\n\nThe five fractional shares total $\\frac{59}{60}$, leaving $\\frac{1}{60}$ of the colony as one bee.",
      "worked": "Let $n$ be the number of bees in the colony.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{n}{6}+\\frac{n}{3}+\\frac{n}{4}+\\frac{n}{5}+\\frac{n}{30}+1&=n\\\\10n+20n+15n+12n+2n+60&=60n\\\\59n+60&=60n\\\\60&=n\\end{align*}$$\nThere are 60 bees in the colony.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 52
  },
